---
description: A catch clause that only rethrows the caught exception has the same effect as omitting the catch altogether and letting it bubble up automatically.
globs:
  - '**/*.cs'
alwaysApply: true
---
# Rule content

<pre>
string s = "";
try
{
s = File.ReadAllText(fileName);
}
catch (Exception e)  // Noncompliant
{
throw;
}
</pre>
 Such clauses should either be removed or populated with the appropriate logic. <pre>
string s = File.ReadAllText(fileName);
</pre>
 or <pre>
string s = "";
try
{
s = File.ReadAllText(fileName);
}
catch (Exception e)
{
logger.LogError(e);
throw;
}
</pre>
 Exceptions This rule will not generate issues for catch blocks if they are followed by a catch block for a more general exception type that does more than just rethrowing the exception. <pre>
var s = ""
try
{
s = File.ReadAllText(fileName);
}
catch (IOException) // Compliant by exception: removing it would change the logic
{
throw;
}
catch (Exception)  // Compliant: does more than just rethrow
{
logger.LogError(e);
throw;
}
</pre>
